{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Dev Bar · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Dev Bar</h1>
    <p>
        The <strong>Via Dev Bar</strong> is a debug overlay built into php-via, like the Symfony Web Debug
        Toolbar or Laravel Debugbar, but built around php-via's signals, scopes and SSE stream. It goes into
        every page without configuration and streams traces and logs as they happen.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
        It is running on this page. Look for the <code>via</code> pill in the bottom-right corner and click it
        open. Every panel below describes what you are looking at.
    </p>

    <h2 id="enabling">Turning it on</h2>
    <p>
        The Dev Bar follows dev mode: on with <code>withDevMode()</code>, off otherwise, and then every
        <code>/_via</code> endpoint answers 404. <code>withDevBar()</code> overrides that either way:
        <code>withDevBar(false)</code> turns it off in dev mode, and <code>withDevBar(true)</code> turns it on
        outside dev mode, read-only, which is how this site shows it.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config = (new Config())
    ->withDevMode($isDev)
    ->withDevBar(true)                 // also outside dev mode, read-only
    ->withDevBarOptions(traces: 100);  // traces kept per worker (default 100)
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <strong>Read-only does not mean private.</strong> With the Dev Bar on, every visitor sees the traces,
        logs, scopes, context ids and signal values of every tab on the worker, also other visitors' tabs, as
        <code>/_via/scopes</code> and the console list them, and the server's request counts and runtime
        figures in the Stats panel. Session scopes show a hash, never the session
        cookie, and traces show <code>[redacted]</code> for attributes named like a password, token, cookie or
        session. Outside development,
        turn it on only for a demo or for a deployment that only trusted people reach, such as one behind an
        admin login.
    </div>
    <p>
        With the Dev Bar off, no trace is recorded and every capture call does nothing. When middleware sets the
        <code>via.csp_nonce</code> request attribute, the Dev Bar's stylesheet and script carry the nonce, as
        <code>via_head</code>'s tags do (see <a href="/docs/twig#csp">Content-Security-Policy</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
        The Dev Bar is separate from the Live Signals panel of php-via's default shell, which shows the page's
        signals in a sidebar in dev mode only.
    </p>

    <h2 id="panels">The seven panels</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Panel</th><th>Shows</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Traces</strong></td><td>A waterfall of every page load, action and broadcast: nested spans with proportional bars, durations and attributes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Signals</strong></td><td>This page's named signals, grouped by scope, with their live values. Editable when writes are on.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>SSE</strong></td><td>The wire: every <code>datastar-fetch</code> event, with the patch it carried (elements or signals) expandable inline.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Request</strong></td><td>Route, context id and the request span's attributes (method, status, byte size).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Scopes</strong></td><td>The worker's scopes, the contexts in each and the connection counts: who shares what.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Stats</strong></td><td>Every figure of <code>getStats()-&gt;getAll()</code>, the broadcast tick, and the hook flags, AIO threads and event loop lag of the worker that answered. See <a href="{{ basePath }}docs/dev-bar#stats">Server statistics</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Logs</strong></td><td>Server log lines, filtered by level, plus errors from the browser. See <a href="{{ basePath }}docs/dev-bar#logs">Server logs</a>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The ⟲ button in the panel header clears the traces and SSE events it shows, and the worker's trace
        buffer with them, for everyone who watches that worker.
    </p>

    <h2 id="spans">Adding your own spans</h2>
    <p>
        php-via traces the request, the action, the render and the broadcast by itself. To time your own work, a
        database query, an HTTP call or an expensive computation, wrap it in <code>$c-&gt;span()</code>. The
        name's prefix before the first dot picks its colour.
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$issues = $c->span('db.list_issues', fn () => $repo->all(), ['limit' => 50]);

// Annotate the span that is open, from anywhere in the action:
$c->traceAttribute('cache.hit', false);
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        A data layer with no <code>Context</code> at hand reaches the tracer directly. It adds to whatever trace
        is open in the current coroutine, and is <code>null</code> while the Dev Bar is off:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
use Mbolli\PhpVia\Tracing\Tracer;

private static function getCell(int $row, int $col): string {
    $tracer = Tracer::current();
    $run = fn (): string => /* the SQLite query */ '';

    return $tracer === null ? $run() : $tracer->span('db.get_cell', $run, ['row' => $row, 'col' => $col]);
}
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        The <a href="{{ basePath }}examples/spreadsheet">Spreadsheet</a> and
        <a href="{{ basePath }}examples/chat-room">Chat Room</a> examples record <code>db.*</code> spans this
        way. Categories map to colours:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Category</th><th>Source</th><th>Colour</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>request</code></td><td>page load and action root spans</td><td>gray</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>render</code></td><td><code>render.regions</code>, <code>render.component</code></td><td>green</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>cache</code></td><td>shared render hits</td><td>yellow</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>db</code></td><td>your <code>db.*</code> spans</td><td>blue</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sse</code></td><td>broadcast fan-out</td><td>violet</td></tr>
            <tr><td>anything else</td><td>your own spans</td><td>teal</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A component's render is a <code>render.component</code> span tagged with the component's namespace, so
        you can see which component rendered again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="logs">Server logs</h2>
    <p>
        Everything that goes through php-via's logger, <code>$app-&gt;log()</code>, broker errors, failing
        actions and php-via's own warnings, also goes to the <strong>Logs</strong> panel as it happens. The
        level filter starts at <code>info</code>; switch to <code>debug</code> for everything or to
        <code>error</code> for failures. <code>window.onerror</code> events from the browser land here too.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
        Only lines that go through php-via's logger show up. <code>error_log()</code> or <code>echo</code> from
        app code bypass it: send those through <code>$app-&gt;log()</code> to see them in the panel.
    </p>

    <h2 id="editing">Editing signals</h2>
    <p>
        The Signals panel can write values back to the server, the way Livewire or React DevTools let you poke
        at state. That needs dev mode and its own switch:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$config
    ->withDevMode(true)
    ->withDevBarOptions(writes: true);   // or set VIA_DEVBAR_WRITES=1
{% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <strong>Writes never work outside dev mode.</strong> <code>withDevBarOptions(writes: true)</code> has no
        effect while dev mode is off, so <code>withDevBar(true)</code> on a public site stays read-only. In dev
        mode, anyone who can reach the page could change <code>ROUTE</code>, <code>SESSION</code> and
        <code>GLOBAL</code> state shared with other users, so keep writes off on a dev server others can reach.
    </div>

    <h2 id="endpoints">Endpoints and the console</h2>
    <p>
        With the Dev Bar on, php-via serves these under <code>/_via</code>; with it off they all answer 404.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Endpoint</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>GET /_via</code></td><td>The Dev Console: the Dev Bar full-screen</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>GET /_via/stream</code></td><td>SSE stream of <code>trace</code> and <code>log</code> events</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>GET /_via/scopes</code></td><td>JSON snapshot for the Scopes panel</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>GET /_via/stats</code></td><td>JSON snapshot for the Stats panel</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>POST /_via/signal</code></td><td>Signal write, refused unless writes are on</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>POST /_via/reset</code></td><td>Clears the worker's trace buffer</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>GET /_via/devbar.js</code>, <code>/_via/devbar.css</code></td><td>The overlay's assets</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The two <code>POST</code> endpoints check the <code>Origin</code> header as actions do. The overlay is a
        web component with its own shadow DOM, so your page's CSS and Datastar do not reach it, and it survives
        morphs of the whole page. Open <a href="{{ basePath }}_via"><code>/_via</code></a> for the console.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
        The trace and log buffers live in each worker, so with <code>withWorkerNum()</code> above 1 the overlay
        and the console show the activity of the worker their stream reached.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stats">Server statistics</h2>
    <p>
        The Stats panel reads <code>/_via/stats</code> every 2&nbsp;s while it is open, and stops when you
        switch to another panel. <code>requests</code>, <code>avg_request_time</code>, <code>actions</code> and
        <code>sse_connections</code> count for the whole server: page, static file and <code>route()</code>
        requests, actions where they run, and streams that opened. The other figures belong to the worker
        that answered, which the panel names, so with several workers they change with the worker a poll
        reaches. Like the other panels, it is there wherever the Dev Bar is, also outside dev mode.
    </p>
    <p>
        In dev mode, <code>GET /_stats</code> returns JSON for the worker that answers: its contexts, the
        connected clients, render timings (<code>render_stats</code>), broadcast flushes
        (<code>broadcast_stats</code>), memory and uptime. Under <code>runtime</code> it lists the
        <code>hook_flags</code> in force, the AIO thread pool (<code>aio_worker_num</code>,
        <code>aio_task_num</code>) and the event loop lag, where a call that blocks the worker shows up. Outside
        dev mode it answers 404, since it lists client IPs. In code, <code>$app-&gt;getStats()</code> returns the
        same render and broadcast figures.
    </p>

    <h2 id="see-also">See also</h2>
    <ul role="list">
        <li><a href="{{ basePath }}docs/development">Development workflow</a>: hot reload and the dev loop</li>
        <li><a href="{{ basePath }}docs/deployment">Deployment</a>: production configuration</li>
        <li><a href="{{ basePath }}docs/design">Design decisions</a>: the runtime model the traces reflect</li>
    </ul>
</article>
{% endblock %}
